-- title:
--   §5: cq scenario equivalence
-- statement:
--   Let $\mathcal N:A'B'\to C$ be a completely positive, trace-preserving channel between finite, positive-dimensional complex quantum registers. Alice and Bob control separate inputs. Codes use independent sender preparations, local encoders, one joint memoryless channel and one joint decoder, with external references untouched and no free shared resources. Fidelity is squared Uhlmann fidelity, trace distance is the full trace norm, and entropies are in bits. Write $CQ(\mathcal N)$ and $Q(\mathcal N)$ for the closed operational classical–quantum and quantum–quantum capacity regions, using joint output fidelity and nonnegative rates.
--
--   For $i=I,II,III$, let $CQ_i(\mathcal N)$ be the closure of achievable cq rates for entanglement generation, entanglement transmission, and strong subspace transmission, respectively. The first two use uniform classical messages and average joint classical/Bell fidelity. The third requires the joint classical/quantum fidelity bound for every message and every pure reference/input state of Bob, permitting private stochastic classical encoding. Then
--   $$CQ_I(\mathcal N)=CQ_{II}(\mathcal N)=CQ_{III}(\mathcal N).$$
--   This identifies the three operational tasks asymptotically, not their exact finite-code performances.
--
--   **Formalization Note** The strong task tests the original message, consistent with the inverse cyclic relabeling on p.25. Extra channel blocks used to establish random seeds count toward the blocklength; there is no free shared seed.
-- source:
--   Capacity Theorems for Quantum Multiple Access Channels: Classical-Quantum and Quantum-Quantum Capacity Regions, arXiv:quant-ph/0501045v3, PDF p. 7, §5: cq scenario equivalence

import Definitions.Def_QAlgorithms_Papers_Independent_Support50_P02

noncomputable section

open scoped BigOperators Matrix ComplexConjugate

namespace QAlgorithms.Papers.Independent.Support50.P02

open QAlgorithms.Papers.Independent.Information100.P67
  (squaredFidelity receiverChannel complementaryChannel IsDegradable)

open QAlgorithms.Papers.Independent.Information100.P21 (Op)

open QAlgorithms.Papers.Independent.Information100.P03 (PureEnsemble)

open QAlgorithms.Papers.Independent.Dependency50.P19 (epr)

attribute [local instance] Classical.propDecidable Classical.decEq

variable {A B C E R S I J : Type*}

variable [Fintype A] [DecidableEq A] [Fintype B] [DecidableEq B]

variable [Fintype C] [DecidableEq C] [Fintype E] [DecidableEq E]

variable [Fintype R] [DecidableEq R] [Fintype S] [DecidableEq S]

variable [Fintype I] [DecidableEq I] [Fintype J] [DecidableEq J]

/-- CQ scenario equivalence, PDF p.7 and §§5.2–5.4, pp.24–29.
The strong criterion tests m, with the p.25 inverse relabeling correction.
Shared seeds in the proof are generated by extra channel blocks and charged;
p.29 derandomization requires existence of a good seed, not every seed. -/
theorem cq_scenarios [Nonempty A] [Nonempty B] [Nonempty C]
    (N : Superop (A × B) C) (hN : IsChannel N) :
    cqMacCapacity N .generation = cqMacCapacity N .transmission ∧
    cqMacCapacity N .transmission = cqMacCapacity N .strong := by sorry

end QAlgorithms.Papers.Independent.Support50.P02

end
